\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Sarah and Tony enjoyed some quality time together, even if \'93enjoyed\'94 mostly meant being bored out of their minds when they weren\'92t uneasy about how both safe and sinister their new daily routine felt. Safe, because they were beset by no PAPER agents, nightclub bombers, Public Affairs police, prophecy facilitators, nor insidious granny fashion-diva arms dealers. Sinister, because that peace depended entirely on squadrons of GI Blues series Elvi, male and female, patrolling the grounds, the perimeter, the hallways, the rooftops, the badly sealed windows, the peanut butter cellar, and under Sarah\'92s bed\'97plus a battalion of tanks that sat like titanic cane toads outside the gates and at strategic street corners.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Perhaps the weirdest part was the Elvi\'92s assumption that Tony was the father of the evil nematode currently squatting in Sarah\'92s uterus. At first, Sarah assumed they\'92d expect them to share a bed, but the Elvi proved to be Catholically opposed to any carnal implications involving Sarah as a \'93Momma.\'94 Where Tony actually slept was a mystery, and Sarah wasn\'92t asking. Though she did idly wonder at times if he was still wearing those fairy boxer shorts.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 When Sarah wasn\'92t bored, she was impatient\'97which, if history is to be told accurately, was a credible percentage of every waking nanosecond and at least half of her nightmares. When the forty-second day since the night of her probable insemination came and went, Sarah feared Kathy would never return, and she\'92d be forced to summon another incarnation of Doctors John and Nick, in the desperate hope the next batch might know what a DNA test was.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy arrived on the forty-third day, toting a piece of medical equipment roughly the size and heft of a sewing machine that had swallowed a briefcase.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Chanta, half a breakfast burrito in hand and the rest smeared across his cheek, padded in bare feet and flannel pyjama bottoms to meet the doorbell. An Elvis guard beat him to it, exchanged words with the preternaturally smiling doctor, then caught a patch she tossed him like a floppy ninja throwing-star.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do not use that with amphetamines,\'94 Kathy cautioned in passing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvis led Kathy upstairs; Chanta followed, trailing a dozen steps behind like an eager but confused puppy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy knocked at Sarah\'92s door, announced herself, and entered at Sarah\'92s muffled invitation. She shut the door in Chanta\'92s face, leaving him floating in a fetid lagoon of hope and apprehension.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvis guarded the door, then sniffed, sneered at Chanta, and demanded, \'93What\'92s in that?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Barramundi,\'94 Chanta said, swallowing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93With onions?\'94 The Elvis nearly vibrated with anxiety.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sure. Want some?\'94 Chanta tempted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvis gagged, pinched his nose, and barked, \'93Nancy!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Soon Nancy barrelled up, lunged for Chanta\'92s breakfast, and scrubbed his face with a tea towel while ignoring his protests. \'93No fish with onions in this house,\'94 she scolded, scouring away every trace of sauce. Satisfied he was now passably presentable to be near Sarah, she vanished.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The door opened abruptly. Kathy appeared, blocking Chanta\'92s path and holding up a syringe like a ceremonial candle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Blood sample,\'94 she said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta licked his burrito-sticky fingers and held out his arm.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Inside Sarah\'92s bedroom, dignity took another small holiday as Kathy performed an invasive, if brief, medical procedure to sample the uterine lodger.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Just a quick jab,\'94 Kathy said, wielding bedside manner like a rusty scalpel.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Sarah reassembled herself while Kathy jiggled the sample in a saline tube, slotted it into the DNA sequencer, and pressed buttons until an icon glowed blue. The machine hummed, grinding like it was mincing peppercorns and window glass.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93How long?\'94 Sarah asked from the bed\'92s edge.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93About ten minutes,\'94 Kathy said, settling in with the air of someone who\'92d done this dance before.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah paced\'97window, bathroom, bed, almost sitting, thinking better of it, bathroom, window, repeat. The DNA sequencer was one of those machines designed above all to go bing! Eventually it did, though to Sarah it felt like ten after work meetings with Ashby-Prynne.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy produced her tablet and started tapping. \'93Comparing the foetal sample with Chanta\'92s blood... just a moment... ah! Well, what do you know?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'94 Sarah\'92s patience was a Home Defence Kettle about to spray Kathy with capsaicin or boil the family budgie.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Chanta\'92s clean. A knight in shining overalls\'97he\'92s not the father.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you sure?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I doubt he\'92s even used the same toothbrush brand; the match is so low it\'92s practically insulting.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stared, hollow as writer\'92s block. \'93Then... how\'97who?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Those weirdos you mentioned\'97the ones from the Memphis Mafia Bar?\'94 Sarah nodded and rummaged through her handbag, producing a business card like evidence at a trial.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Seven Sages Corporation,\'94 Kathy read, scrolling her tablet. \'93Let\'92s see... Kleoboulos Windus\'85 yes\'85 Pittacus d\'92Lesbos?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Don\'92t recall. Maybe one on hiatus.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Solon Athena?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Solo, yes\'97and Chilo too.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Chilon Damagetus?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s hope spiked. \'93That\'92s him. The nasty one.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Okay, let\'92s see if there\'92s DNA\'85\'94 Kathy ran comparisons, waited for her tablet\'92s chime\'97a small-prize-in-a-large-lottery sound. \'93Sketchy records, but unlikely any of them match.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah collapsed onto the bed, deflated. \'93So I\'92m screwed.\'94 She buried her face in the pillow.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not yet.\'94 Kathy tapped and long-pressed. \'93I\'92ll run the sample through the data swamps. This might take a while.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92ve got nothing but a while. The Elvis Guard isn\'92t letting me go anywhere.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy paused mid-tap, her face contorting in animated surprise. \'93While we wait... let me check another assumption.\'94 She peeled off Sarah\'92s patch, tapped the sequencer again, and waited.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is something wrong?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Wrong? No. Science never lies, it just surprises the hell out of you.\'94 Kathy raised a silencing hand as results appeared. \'93This is really weird.\'94 She let out a hysterical laugh, then sobered, fixing Sarah with a sincere stare. \'93You are not the mother.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s face contorted. \'93So those taxing Sages implanted someone else\'92s embryo in me?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy\'92s jaw set. \'93Appears so.\'94 She put down her tablet and hugged Sarah. \'93At least now you know\'97there\'92s no reason to keep it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah felt empty, though her body was fuller than she\'92d prefer. \'93Tell me everything about this thing inside me.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Hours later, after more tablet chimes and data swamping, Kathy woke Sarah from an exhausted doze. \'93I\'92ve got answers,\'94 she said, hair mussed, eyes bright with discovery and pharmaceuticals.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Sarah sat up, grimacing through dizziness. \'93Tell me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Your uterine houseguest has a lot of low-percentage DNA matches\'97like the father is an ancestor to hundreds of people, going back generations.\'94 Kathy scrolled through messages. \'93My colleague Constance ran additional analysis. The DNA is perfect\'97absolutely zero copying errors. Viral editing evident, corrected flaws, optimal development. It\'92s a clone.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s jaw set. \'93What did those creeps do to me? Am I their science experiment?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy took her hand. \'93You\'92ve been violated worse than any everyday assault,\'94 she said. \'93But we\'92ll figure this out. Together.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just tell me whose clone I\'92m carrying.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy nodded, opened a message. \'93Clinton Richard Dawkins, born Nairobi, 1941. Evolutionary biologist.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What\'92s that?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Someone who spent his life arguing against religion, apparently. He claimed teaching religion in schools was child abuse.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah snorted. \'93Good luck with that. The Church of Latter Day Capitalists own Ulyssia.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93All his books were banned in the Great Enlightenment. Why would the Sages want a clone of this guy?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah remembered: \'93
\f2\i The prophecy requires a child be born
\f1\i0 . Chilo said they\'92re bringing about the end of the universe.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93First step in every successful religion,\'94 Kathy said, applying a fresh patch. \'93Tell people they\'92re going to die sooner than usual.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah managed a brittle laugh. \'93I asked what to call their Antichrist. They said to call him Richard.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Outside, Chanta paced the hallway, still tasting barramundi and wondering if Sarah would ever look at him the same way again, now that she knew he wasn\'92t the father of the clone that would end the universe.
\f0\fs24 <!$Scr_Ps::1>}